  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037675/assets/f59f65e184ccf4fdf2ae2124543ece45d41afce09d743dec9f17730dd00124d4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ of the circle above is increasing. The circumference and radius of the circle are $C$ and $r$ respectively. \nFind the rate of change of the area of the circle with respect to its circumference.",
  "worked_solution": "The area of a circle is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the circle.\n\nWe know that $C=2\\pi r.$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the circumference $C$ by substituting $r=\\frac{C}{2\\pi }$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{\u2032}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}({C}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }(2C) \\\\  & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
